<h1 class="article-title">Power BI Reporting Services Explained: Power BI Service vs Report Server vs SSRS</h1>
<p class="article-dek">Three Microsoft reporting options with confusingly similar names. Here is what each one does, how they differ and how to pick the right one.</p>
<div class="article-byline"><div class="article-avatar" style="background:#4C9C8B;">NK</div><div><div class="article-author">Nathalie</div><div class="article-role">Consultant, Tycoon Clap</div></div><div class="article-time">12 min read</div></div>
<div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="20" width="184" height="120" rx="14" fill="#DCEFE9"/><rect x="44" y="34" width="152" height="92" rx="10" fill="#fff"/><rect x="56" y="44" width="110" height="14" rx="7" fill="#E4E8F2"/><rect x="56" y="70" width="128" height="14" rx="5" fill="#357566"/><rect x="56" y="90" width="100" height="10" rx="5" fill="#DCEFE9"/><rect x="56" y="106" width="116" height="10" rx="5" fill="#DCEFE9"/></svg></div>
<div class="article-grid">
<article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
<p>Microsoft offers several ways to publish and share reports, and the names overlap enough to confuse almost everyone. People search "reporting services power bi" because they are trying to work out whether those words describe one product or three.</p>
<div class="stat-callout"><div class="stat-callout-icon"><svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg></div><p>Short answer: Power BI Service is the cloud option, Power BI Report Server is the on-premises option, and SSRS is the older on-premises tool built mainly for paginated, print-style reports. Microsoft now steers new on-premises work toward Power BI Report Server.</p></div>
<p>This guide compares the three side by side, explains paginated vs interactive reports, covers licensing at a high level and gives a simple way to decide. If you are still planning what to measure, start with <a href="dashboards-are-not-strategy-why-data-without-decisions-is-useless.html">dashboards are not strategy</a>, and for hands-on help see our <a href="Data-analytics-services-usa.html">data analytics services</a>.</p><h2 id="terms">The three options in plain English</h2><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Where it runs</th><th>What it is best known for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Power BI Service</td><td>Microsoft cloud (app.powerbi.com)</td><td>Interactive dashboards, sharing, collaboration and scheduled refresh</td></tr><tr><td>Power BI Report Server</td><td>Your own servers (on-premises)</td><td>Power BI reports plus paginated reports, kept inside your network</td></tr><tr><td>SSRS (SQL Server Reporting Services)</td><td>Your own servers (on-premises)</td><td>Pixel-perfect paginated reports such as invoices and statements</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The phrase "power bi and reporting services" can also refer to using both together, for example Power BI for interactive analysis and paginated reports for formal documents.</p><h2 id="service">Power BI Service: the cloud option</h2><p>Power BI Service is Microsoft's hosted platform. You build reports in Power BI Desktop, publish them to workspaces and share them through apps, links or embedded views. Microsoft runs the infrastructure, so there are no servers for you to patch or scale.</p><ul class="check-list"><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Strengths:</strong> frequent feature releases, easy sharing, mobile apps, row-level security, AI features and integration with the wider Microsoft ecosystem.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Limits:</strong> data and reports live in the cloud, which some regulated organizations cannot allow, and licensing is per user or per capacity.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Best for:</strong> most businesses that are comfortable using the cloud and want the newest capabilities.</span></li></ul><h2 id="rs">Power BI Report Server: the on-premises option</h2><p>Power BI Report Server lets you publish Power BI reports and paginated reports to a server you control. It includes everything SSRS offers, plus Power BI reports built in a special version of Power BI Desktop for Report Server.</p><ul class="check-list"><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Strengths:</strong> data stays on your own infrastructure, which helps with strict compliance, air-gapped networks and data residency rules.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Limits:</strong> it receives fewer features than the cloud service, updates arrive on a slower schedule and some cloud-only capabilities, such as dashboards and many AI features, are not included.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Best for:</strong> organizations that must keep data on premises and still want modern Power BI reports.</span></li></ul><h2 id="ssrs">SSRS: the classic paginated reporting tool</h2><p>SQL Server Reporting Services has been around for many years. It specializes in paginated reports: fixed-layout documents designed to print or export cleanly to PDF or Excel, such as invoices, statements, regulatory filings and operational lists that run across many pages.</p><p>Microsoft has indicated that SQL Server 2022 is the last release with a standalone SSRS, and that Power BI Report Server is the path forward for on-premises reporting. Existing SSRS installations keep working, but new projects should plan around Power BI Report Server. Check Microsoft's current documentation for support dates before you decide.</p><h2 id="types">Paginated vs interactive reports</h2><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Paginated reports</th><th>Interactive Power BI reports</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Print-ready, fixed-layout documents</td><td>Exploration, filtering and visual analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Typical examples</td><td>Invoices, statements, compliance reports</td><td>Sales dashboards, KPI scorecards</td></tr><tr><td>Layout</td><td>Exact placement across multiple pages</td><td>Flexible, screen-based visuals</td></tr><tr><td>User interaction</td><td>Parameters and drill-through</td><td>Slicers, cross-filtering and drill-down</td></tr><tr><td>Built with</td><td>Power BI Report Builder or SSRS tools</td><td>Power BI Desktop</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="article-pull">Most organizations need both: interactive reports to find the answer, paginated reports to deliver the official version.</div><h2 id="compare">Power BI Service vs Report Server vs SSRS: side by side</h2><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Power BI Service</th><th>Power BI Report Server</th><th>SSRS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Microsoft cloud</td><td>Your servers</td><td>Your servers</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive Power BI reports</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Paginated reports</td><td>Yes, with Premium or Fabric capacity</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Dashboards and AI features</td><td>Full set</td><td>Limited</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Update pace</td><td>Monthly, continuous</td><td>Several times per year</td><td>Slow, legacy</td></tr><tr><td>Infrastructure effort</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Future direction</td><td>Primary Microsoft focus</td><td>On-premises successor to SSRS</td><td>Being folded into Report Server</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="licensing">Licensing at a glance</h2><p>Licensing changes often, so verify current terms with Microsoft before budgeting. In general:</p><ul><li><strong>Power BI Service:</strong> priced per user (Pro or Premium Per User) or by capacity through Microsoft Fabric or Premium. Viewing shared content may require a license unless the content sits on eligible capacity.</li><li><strong>Power BI Report Server:</strong> available through Power BI Premium capacity licensing or through SQL Server Enterprise Edition with active Software Assurance.</li><li><strong>SSRS:</strong> included with SQL Server licensing, with features varying by edition.</li></ul><h2 id="choose">How to choose: a simple decision guide</h2><ul class="check-list"><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Choose Power BI Service if</strong> you can use the cloud and want the fastest path to modern, shareable dashboards.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Choose Power BI Report Server if</strong> regulations or policy require on-premises hosting but you still want Power BI reports.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Keep SSRS if</strong> you already run stable paginated reports and have no near-term reason to migrate, then plan a move to Report Server.</span></li><li><svg class="check-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><strong>Use a hybrid</strong> if sensitive data must stay on premises while other reporting lives in the cloud.</span></li></ul><h2 id="migrate">Migrating from SSRS to Power BI</h2><ol><li><strong>Inventory your reports.</strong> List every report, its owner, usage and data source, and retire what nobody opens.</li><li><strong>Classify them.</strong> Mark each as paginated, interactive or both.</li><li><strong>Move paginated reports first.</strong> RDL files can often be reused with limited changes.</li><li><strong>Rebuild high-value reports as interactive.</strong> Redesign for decisions, not just data display.</li><li><strong>Test security and refresh.</strong> Confirm permissions, schedules and data gateways.</li><li><strong>Train users and set governance.</strong> Define who can publish, certify and edit.</li></ol><div class="stat-callout"><div class="stat-callout-icon"><svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#357566" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg></div><p>Whichever tool you pick, reports only help when the metrics behind them are defined and tracked. See <a href="the-real-cost-of-not-tracking-your-marketing-funnel.html">the real cost of not tracking your marketing funnel</a>.</p></div>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div class="article-faq"><details open><summary>What is the difference between Power BI Service and Power BI Report Server?</summary><p class="faq-a">Power BI Service is Microsoft's cloud platform with the newest features and easy sharing. Power BI Report Server runs on your own servers, keeps data on premises and receives fewer, slower updates.</p></details><details><summary>Is Power BI Report Server the same as SSRS?</summary><p class="faq-a">No, but it includes SSRS capabilities. It publishes paginated reports like SSRS and also hosts interactive Power BI reports. Microsoft positions it as the successor for on-premises reporting.</p></details><details><summary>Is SSRS being discontinued?</summary><p class="faq-a">Microsoft has indicated that SQL Server 2022 is the last release with standalone SSRS and that Power BI Report Server is the path forward. Existing installations continue to work, but you should check current support dates and plan a migration.</p></details><details><summary>What are paginated reports in Power BI?</summary><p class="faq-a">Paginated reports are fixed-layout, print-ready documents such as invoices and statements. They are built with Power BI Report Builder and available in the Power BI Service with eligible capacity, and in Power BI Report Server.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need Power BI Premium for Report Server?</summary><p class="faq-a">Power BI Report Server is available with Power BI Premium capacity or with SQL Server Enterprise Edition and active Software Assurance. Confirm current licensing terms with Microsoft.</p></details><details><summary>Which should a small business choose?</summary><p class="faq-a">Most small businesses are best served by Power BI Service because it needs no servers and updates continuously. On-premises options make sense mainly when compliance or policy requires data to stay in-house.</p></details></div>
<p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Licensing, pricing and support details change. Information on this page is general guidance, not a quote from Tycoon Clap or Microsoft, so confirm current terms with Microsoft before deciding.</p>
